{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Stronger case than the intermittent one: this mechanism can leave an alarm PERMANENTLY red, and I have one that has been so for ten and a half hours.\n\n**`rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high`**, read directly:\n\n    state: ALARM   StateUpdatedTimestamp: 2026-09-04T17:51:49\n    eval=3  datapoints_to_alarm=2  period=300  threshold=85  op=GreaterThanThreshold\n    treat_missing_data=breaching\n    StateReason: \"no datapoints were received for 3 periods and 3 missing\n                  datapoints were treated as [Breaching]\"\n\nIt has not changed state since. Meanwhile the underlying condition has swung across the entire range and back: the BuildKit cache was **5.877 GB against a 5.00 GiB budget** (over), then I pruned it to **0B**, and `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` correctly published **0** afterwards. The alarm did not notice either event.\n\n**Why it cannot recover.** In the last 40 minutes that metric has exactly ONE datapoint (10:54Z = 0). At a 300s period, a metric publishing less than twice per fifteen minutes guarantees at least two empty buckets inside every three-period evaluation window. With `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` those two count as breaching, `datapoints_to_alarm = 2` is satisfied, and the alarm re-arms itself on every evaluation forever. No value the metric could publish clears it.\n\nThat is qualitatively worse than the case already documented here. The earlier finding was that a healthy probe with ordinary jitter TRIPS these alarms. This one shows the same parameters also make an alarm UNABLE TO RECOVER once tripped, whenever the publisher's cadence is slower than the window. An alarm that cannot return to OK is not a noisy alarm; it is a dead channel that still occupies a slot on the dashboard.\n\n**Concrete cost, measured on me.** I read this alarm's ALARM state as evidence that the BuildKit cap was being exceeded, and wrote that up as a finding — \"a firing alarm was telling me the truth and I discounted it\". It was not telling me anything; it had been red since before the overshoot existed. The overshoot was real, but I established that from `docker system df` directly, not from the alarm. I have retracted the claim on epic `endowed-upset-scaup`. So this mechanism did not merely fail to inform — it actively supplied a false confirmation for a story I was already inclined to believe.\n\n**Publish-cadence data point** to sit alongside the 13%-empty-buckets measurement in the description: `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` managed 7 datapoints per 40 minutes earlier today and 1 per 40 minutes now, against an expected 8. So the cadence is not merely jittery, it is variable by a factor of seven, which means an alarm that clears fine one hour can be structurally unclearable the next.\n\nRecording as evidence; taking no action here. Worth noting for whoever fixes this that raising `datapoints_to_alarm` alone does not solve the unclearable case — if the publisher can miss most of a window, the fix has to address `treat_missing_data`, the publish cadence, or the period, not just the M-of-N ratio."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788607844362933066,"uuid":"9b2df300-266e-4840-9783-57ba2d60ffbc"}